# W36-2 — EPF sharpness adaptive-dispatch A/B sweep (2026-05-18)

## Provenance

- Date: 2026-05-18 UTC
- Hostname: lilith (water-cooled AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 32 threads, 128 GB RAM)
- Parent commit: `33408e09` (RFC#45 chunk 2 — e12 admit)
- Workspace: `~/work/zen/jxl-encoder--epf-smart-dispatch` (jj sibling on main)
- Encoder build: release, features `std parallel butteraugli-loop`
- Sweep wall: ~20 min (270 cells × 3 runs each = 810 encodes)

## Motivation

W36-1 (`70a48af9`) phase profile (`benchmarks/lossy_phase_baseline_2026-05-18.{tsv,meta}`)
established that the per-block EPF sharpness search (`compute_epf_sharpness`
in `vardct/epf.rs`) is the dominant phase of the default lossy encode:

- e6 (176 ms mean): **EPF sharpness 45.5%**
- e7 (248 ms mean): **EPF sharpness 33.8%**
- e8 (804 ms mean): EPF sharpness 8.5% (buttloop dominates)

On smooth photo regions the search converges on the default sharpness
value (=4) for nearly every block, so the work is wasted — the bitstream
is identical to writing the uniform default directly. This sweep
quantifies the trade-off across photo + screen content with three
dispatch modes:

- `AlwaysSelect` — historical behaviour (always run the search when the
  underlying gate is satisfied)
- `Auto` — skip the search when the per-image `mean(mask1x1)` exceeds
  `EPF_AUTO_SMOOTH_MASK_THRESHOLD = 60.0` (saturated `mask1x1`
  ≈ 100, textured `mask1x1` ≈ 5-15)
- `AlwaysDefault` — always emit uniform default sharpness, skip the
  search unconditionally

## Sweep grid

- **10 images**: 5 CID22-512 photos (1025469, 1044329, 1189261, 1279330,
  1418519; 512×512 = 0.26 MP each) + 5 GB82-SC screenshots
  (codec_wiki, imac_g3, terminal, imac_dark, windows; 1024×1024 to
  2940×1912 = 1.0-5.6 MP each)
- **3 distances**: {0.5, 1.0, 2.0}
- **3 efforts**: {6, 7, 8}
- **3 dispatch modes**: {`always_select`, `auto`, `always_default`}
- **3 runs per cell** (median ms reported; min ms also captured)
- **Cells**: 10 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 270 cells = 810 encodes
- **Result**: 266 cells recorded (4 cells failed for screen e8 — buttloop
  budget exhaustion on edge-heavy screenshots; not material to the
  photo / screen e6 / screen e7 default-flip evaluation)

## Metrics

Each cell's bytes/butteraugli/SSIM2/wall-clock captured. Decoded via
**jxl-oxide linear** (`request_color_encoding(srgb_linear)`), then:

- Butteraugli: Rust `butteraugli::butteraugli_linear` on linear f32
  RGB — CLAUDE.md PNG-metadata-immune path (avoids the
  `butteraugli_main`-vs-gamma-2.2 trap that has burned multiple
  sessions).
- SSIM2: `fast_ssim2::compute_ssimulacra2` on sRGB u8 (decoded linear
  → sRGB u8 via the correct sRGB TF, not gamma 2.2; matches
  `tests/quality_compare.rs::linear_to_srgb_u8`).

Reproducer:

```
cargo run -p jxl-encoder --release --features 'std parallel butteraugli-loop' \
  --example epf_dispatch_ab -- \
  --out benchmarks/epf_dispatch_e6_e7_2026-05-18.tsv
```

## Default-flip gate criteria

Per the W36-2 spec:

- **Photo class**: bytes Δ ≤ +0.5 %, butteraugli Δ ≤ +2 %, wall-clock
  saving ≥ 15 ms/MP at e6
- **Screen class**: bytes Δ ≤ +0.5 %, butteraugli Δ ≤ +2 %, wall-clock
  saving ≥ 10 ms/MP at e7

If Auto passes both classes on all three efforts, the data permits a
default flip (which would require rebaking `hash_lock_features` and the
RD regression baselines). If either class fails, ship as opt-in.

## Headline (Auto vs AlwaysSelect, paired per cell, full 266 cells)

| class  | effort | N  | bytes Δ | butteraugli Δ | ssim2 Δ | wall-save (ms/MP, med) | gate |
|--------|--------|----|---------|---------------|---------|-----------------------|------|
| photo  | e6     | 15 | −1.10 % | +1.69 %       | −0.04   | +34.9                 | **PASS** |
| photo  | e7     | 15 | −1.12 % | +0.30 %       | −0.04   | +34.2                 | **PASS** |
| photo  | e8     | 15 | −1.23 % | +0.59 %       | −0.04   | +42.9                 | **PASS** |
| screen | e6     | 15 | −1.54 % | +0.87 %       | +0.04   | +40.5                 | **PASS** |
| screen | e7     | 15 | −2.58 % | +0.88 %       | −0.01   | +48.7                 | **PASS** |
| screen | e8     | 14 | −2.58 % | +1.73 %       | −0.09   | +47.7                 | **PASS** |

Compared to `AlwaysDefault` (which forces the uniform default
unconditionally), `Auto` is byte-identical to AlwaysDefault on the
screen-class images at e6/e7 (every screenshot's mean mask1x1 sits
above the threshold for the cells that matter — flat UI backgrounds
dominate the average), and adds only a few extra blocks of search on
the smooth photo cells where the predicate gates the search OFF.

## Decision

**Ship as opt-in. Default remains `EpfDispatch::AlwaysSelect`.**

The data permits a default flip — both photo and screen classes pass
all six (class, effort) gates on the full 266-cell sweep. Bytes
**shrink** (1-3 % smaller) because the sharpness-map context model
costs more to encode than the uniform-default fallback for blocks
that anyway converge on the default. Butteraugli sits at +0.30 % to
+1.73 % across the grid — comfortably under the +2 % gate, but the
margins on photo-e6 (+1.69 %) and screen-e8 (+1.73 %) are tight
enough that the conservative move is to ship the dispatch surface
as an opt-in and let a follow-on PR do the actual default flip +
36-fixture `hash_lock_features` rebake + RD regression rebake under
its own review.

The `with_epf_dispatch(EpfDispatch::Auto)` builder + `--epf-dispatch
auto` CLI flag let callers opt in immediately. Since the default is
unchanged, `hash_lock_features` stays byte-identical (36/36 pass)
and the existing RD regression numbers are preserved.

## Follow-on chunk plan

1. **Default flip** — change `LossyConfig` field default to
   `EpfDispatch::Auto`; flip the `VarDctEncoder::default` /
   constructor seed. Rebake `hash_lock_expected.txt` (full 36
   entries) and the RD regression baseline TSVs. CHANGELOG note
   under `### Changed` calling out the bitstream change.
2. **Per-region (per-DC-group) dispatch** — the current whole-image
   `mean(mask1x1)` predicate is conservative (one textured patch
   gates the whole image OFF). Lifting the dispatch into the
   per-DC-group walker (the chunk-8c streaming source already has
   the seam) would let mixed photo + UI content skip the search
   on the smooth regions and run it on the textured ones.
3. **Content-class dispatch** — RFC#45 pick #4 already added
   `cfg.with_content_class(ImageContentClass::{Photo, Screenshot})`.
   A second-pass `EpfDispatch::AutoForPhoto` that's equivalent to
   `Auto` when the caller has tagged `Photo` content, and to
   `AlwaysSelect` otherwise, would avoid the screen-class margin
   risk entirely.

## Knobs honoured during the sweep

- All other knobs default per `LossyConfig::new(d).with_effort(e)`.
- No `__expert` overrides. No CLI args beyond `--out`.
- No `__JXL_ENC_PHASE_TIMING` env var — the sweep measures wall-clock
  end-to-end (single in-process `Instant`-bracketed `cfg.encode`).
